  {
   "id": "sigint",
   "title": "C++ program that shuts down cleanly on SIGINT",
   "steps": [
    {
     "kind": "exercise",
     "heading": "Exercise",
     "minutes": 25,
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "p",
       "c": [
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": "From a blank file, in 25 minutes, write a program that:"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "ol",
       "items": [
        [
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": "loops doing \"work\": it counts ticks and sleeps 100 ms per tick;"
         }
        ],
        [
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": "on SIGINT or SIGTERM, stops the loop;"
         }
        ],
        [
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": "then prints how many ticks ran, prints "
         },
         {
          "t": "code",
          "v": "saved"
         },
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": " to stand for clean-up, and exits with status 0."
         }
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "c": [
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": "Test it:"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "ul",
       "items": [
        [
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": "Run it and press Ctrl+C after a second; "
         },
         {
          "t": "code",
          "v": "echo $?"
         },
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": " must print 0."
         }
        ],
        [
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": "Start it again, find its PID with "
         },
         {
          "t": "code",
          "v": "pgrep"
         },
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": ", and stop it from a second terminal with "
         },
         {
          "t": "code",
          "v": "kill -TERM"
         },
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": "."
         }
        ],
        [
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": "Then try "
         },
         {
          "t": "code",
          "v": "kill -KILL"
         },
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": " and see that "
         },
         {
          "t": "code",
          "v": "saved"
         },
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": " never appears."
         }
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "h",
       "c": [
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": "Solution"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "c": [
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": "In this version, the program raises SIGINT itself after three ticks, so it can run unattended. When you run your own, press Ctrl+C instead."
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "code",
       "lang": "cpp",
       "tag": "run",
       "expect": "",
       "text": "#include <atomic>\n#include <chrono>\n#include <csignal>\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <thread>\n\nstatic std::atomic<bool> stop_requested{false};\nstatic_assert(std::atomic<bool>::is_always_lock_free, \"a signal handler may only touch lock-free atomics\");\n\n// The only thing the handler does is set a flag: anything else is unsafe here.\nextern \"C\" void on_signal(int) { stop_requested.store(true); }\n\nint main() {\n  std::signal(SIGINT, on_signal);\n  std::signal(SIGTERM, on_signal);\n  int ticks = 0;\n  while (!stop_requested.load()) {\n    ++ticks;\n    std::printf(\"tick %d\\n\", ticks);\n    std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(100));\n    if (ticks == 3) std::raise(SIGINT);  // stands in for Ctrl+C so the example stops on its own\n  }\n  std::printf(\"stopping after %d ticks\\n\", ticks);\n  std::puts(\"saved\");  // clean-up happens here, in normal code, not in the handler\n  return 0;\n}"
      },
      {
       "t": "output",
       "text": "tick 1\ntick 2\ntick 3\nstopping after 3 ticks\nsaved"
      },
      {
       "t": "p",
       "c": [
        {
         "t": "text",
         "v": "Points to check in your own version:"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "ul",
       "items": [
        [
         {
          "t": "b",
          "v": "The flag type."
         },
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": " It is a lock-free "
         },
         {
          "t": "code",
          "v": "std::atomic<bool>"
         },
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": ", or "
         },
         {
          "t": "code",
          "v": "volatile std::sig_atomic_t"
         },
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": ". A plain "
         },
         {
          "t": "code",
          "v": "bool"
         },
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": " is a data race: the compiler may keep it in a register and never see the change."
         }
        ],
        [
         {
          "t": "b",
          "v": "The work in the handler."
         },
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": " There is none apart from setting the flag."
         }
        ],
        [
         {
          "t": "b",
          "v": "`sleep_for` and signals."
         },
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": " A signal ends a sleeping system call early on Linux, but "
         },
         {
          "t": "code",
          "v": "sleep_for"
         },
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": " goes back to sleep for the rest of the time. So the loop may take up to one tick longer to notice the flag. That's fine for 100 ms ticks."
         }
        ],
        [
         {
          "t": "b",
          "v": "The better API."
         },
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": " "
         },
         {
          "t": "code",
          "v": "sigaction"
         },
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": " is the precise API for installing handlers. "
         },
         {
          "t": "code",
          "v": "std::signal"
         },
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": " is enough on Linux, where the handler stays installed."
         }
        ]
       ]
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "traps",
   "title": "Zombie and orphan processes; unsafe work in signal handlers",
   "steps": [
    {
     "kind": "failure",
     "heading": "Failure cases",
     "minutes": 10,
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "ul",
       "items": [
        [
         {
          "t": "b",
          "v": "Zombie."
         },
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": " A child has exited, but its parent never called "
         },
         {
          "t": "code",
          "v": "wait()"
         },
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": ". It shows as "
         },
         {
          "t": "code",
          "v": "Z"
         },
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": " or "
         },
         {
          "t": "code",
          "v": "<defunct>"
         },
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": " in "
         },
         {
          "t": "code",
          "v": "ps"
         },
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": ". It uses no memory, but it holds its PID. A long-running parent that starts children and never waits for them leaks PIDs until the system can't start processes. The fix is for the parent to wait for its children, or to handle SIGCHLD."
         }
        ],
        [
         {
          "t": "b",
          "v": "Orphan."
         },
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": " The parent died first. The child is adopted by PID 1, or by a \"subreaper\" such as systemd, which waits for it when it ends. That's harmless, but it means that killing a launcher doesn't necessarily stop what it started."
         }
        ],
        [
         {
          "t": "b",
          "v": "Unsafe work in a handler."
         },
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": " A signal can arrive in the middle of any function, including in the middle of "
         },
         {
          "t": "code",
          "v": "printf"
         },
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": " or "
         },
         {
          "t": "code",
          "v": "malloc"
         },
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": ", which hold internal locks. If the handler calls the same function, the process can deadlock or corrupt memory. Only "
         },
         {
          "t": "i",
          "v": "async-signal-safe"
         },
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": " functions are allowed in a handler: "
         },
         {
          "t": "code",
          "v": "write"
         },
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": ", "
         },
         {
          "t": "code",
          "v": "_exit"
         },
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": " and a few others. "
         },
         {
          "t": "code",
          "v": "printf"
         },
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": ", "
         },
         {
          "t": "code",
          "v": "new"
         },
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": ", "
         },
         {
          "t": "code",
          "v": "std::cout"
         },
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": ", locking a mutex and "
         },
         {
          "t": "code",
          "v": "exit"
         },
         {
          "t": "text",
          "v": " are not. Set a flag, and do everything else in normal code:"
         }
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "t": "code",
       "lang": "cpp",
       "tag": "fragment",
       "expect": "",
